\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This manual describes the usage of a massively-automated 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

case template
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 for single-cylinder engine 3D-CFD using ESI's OpenFOAM.
 It provides a dedicated means of simulating 3D turbulent flow including
 - but not limited to - multi-species flow, spray injection, and combustion
 phenomena in a single-cylinder spark-ignited engine of quasi-arbitrary
 geometry with or without passive prechamber.
 The template is written in BASH (Bourne Again Shell) and designed to run
 under Linux operating systems (OS), e.g.
 Ubuntu-based ones such as Canonical's Ubuntu 22.04 LTS, ZORIN OS 16 and
 others (the template has been tested on the mentioned OS).
 Suitable computational platforms range from powerful personal computers
 (PC), through Beowulf-type local area network (LAN) clusters to cloud-based
 or local HPC (High-Performance Computing) platforms.
 Most tasks in the template are performed on the 
\shape italic
main
\shape default
 or 
\shape italic
gateway
\shape default
 PC (main node), which - ideally - is a well-powered machine immediately
 accessible to the user for geometry and case set-up as well as launching,
 post-processing, and archiving each simulation.
 The smaller chunk of tasks basically covers only the flow computations
 themselves which, natrually, demand vast computational ressources for computati
on parallelisation, i.e.
 predominantly a high number of computing cores.
 Hence, the template is designed to optionally allow to outsource the flow
 computations to other platforms automatically during case runtime.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At this point it is worth noting that, in general, each case is being automatica
lly sub-divided by the template into non-uniform crank angle interals during
 runtim
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Only the computationally more expensive flow calculations are parallelised
 non-locally (LAN, cloud) - if selected so by the user -, whereby these
 calculations are controlled and performed automatically through the applet.
 For ease of use, the themplate is further designed in a way that it only
 requires minimal user interaction and similarly limited input data in the
 form of strictly defined
\end_layout

