 that is available and suitable)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If engine configuration and case settings are defined correctly and sound,
 the applet only needs to be launched once by the user and will then run,
 post-process and finish the case fully autonomously without demanding any
 further supervision or user-intervention.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
System Requirements
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The following hardware and software requirements have shown to work fairly
 well in a general sense but other hardware configurations might also perform
 sufficiently or even better.
 It is nevertheless recommended not to switch to other shell languages than
 BASH or to use operating systems that do not natively support BASH, for
 compatibility reasons.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Hardware
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A multi-core PC with significant memory capacity (both RAM and ROM) as well
 as decent graphics processing power is recommended as main node.
 This is due to
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the practical need to parallelise (at least) mesh generation, mesh checks
 and other processes (4 CPU/threads is often a reasonable minimum, more
 may be better),
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
very high occasional demands in RAM (min.
 of 32 GB is recommended, more is better) for mesh generation during template
 runtime, and
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
huge amounts of results data ranging in the tens to hundrets of GB (min.
 500 GB, more is better).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For read/write speed, the OS should be installed on SSD (Solid State Disk).
 SSD should also be used as local storage medium to speed up, e.g., results
 data processing on the main node.
 Pre-processing of CAD data as well as post-processing of obtained results
 further require high-performance graphics hardware.
 
\end_layout

