\begin_layout Subsection
Applet Installation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For ease of use and to prevent the user from overloading their main node
 with, e.g., redundant input data, it is recommended to install the template
 via installation script.
 For each engine configuration
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
An 
\shape italic
engine configuration
\shape default
 is here understood as a unique combination of geometries for piston, valves
 etc.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 one then has to create only a single 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

centralised
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and permanent configuration folder which the applet will select and browse
 during runtime.
 During case set-up the user therefore only needs to provide the unique
 configuration tag (e.g.
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\family typewriter
scaniaDC1674A_prechamberV40
\family default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, i.e.
 a descriptive name for the configuration's input data folder) which enables
 the applet to find all required information autonomously.
 The top-level input data source folder has to be defined during installation
 and will be referred to as environment variable 
\family typewriter
$INPUT
\family default
 in the following.
 After successful installation, all below mentioned applet functions are
 available on the respective machine as terminal commands.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Applet Usage
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The most important aspect in using the here described templated case applet
 is to provide it with every piece of input data exactly as explained below.
 For instance, missing objects (e.g.
 geometry) or poor input data quality will likely lead to unexpected behaviour
 of the applet, certainly including software crashes or else.
 It is thus strongly recommended to read this manual completely, follow
 the below steps in every mentioned detail faithfully, and not to skip or
 interchange any of them.
\end_layout

