\begin_layout Subsection
Surface-Wrapping and Geometry Pre-Processing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To acutally create an engine configuration from scratch, a process called
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

surface-wrapping
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 is necessary once per engine definition.
 In that process the user 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

manually
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 reconstructs the bounding surfaces of the flow domain, in most cases based
 on respective STEP/IGES files of cylinder heads etc, similar to Figure
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:STL-geometry-components"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
 The use of parametric CAD data (original CAD files) is generally not recommende
d because this tends to inflate and complicate the input data source unnecessari
ly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename img/engineItems
	scale 50

\end_inset


\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Mandatory STL geometry components of an engine model
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:STL-geometry-components"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The reconstructed surfaces must cover the entire domain for all possible
 engine states, which may be understood as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

domain prototypes
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in the sense of valve-openness permutations:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Intake
\series default
: Only intake valves opened (fresh charge is being sucked into the cylinder)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Exhaust
\series default
: Only exhaust valves opened (exhaust gas is being expelled from the cylinder)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Closed
\series default
: All valves are closed (gas compression, ignition and combustion take place)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Overlap
\series default
: All valves are opened (intake and exhaust valves are opened simultaneously
 for improved cylinder flushing)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
How these prototypes have to be respected during the surface-wrapping process
 will be explained later.
 For now it is only worthwhile knowing that for an engine with valve overlap,
 4 distinct states - the domain prototypes - will be created by the applet.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The reconstructed surface geometry required to assemble the domain prototypes
 must be stored in a strictly defined manner to make sure that the applet
 finds each surface patch (e.g.
 for piston, valves or spark plug) while running.
 STL (Stereolithography) is the here favoured data format for the reconstructed
 surfaces, which represents simple unstructured 2D triangulations of a given
 shape.
 STL data can be ASCII- or binary encoded, the former being human-readable,
 larger in size (memory) and takes the machine longer to write and read
 while the latter, by contrast, is not human-readable but smaller in size
 and quicker to read and write.
 An ASCII-encoded STL file looks like the following:
\end_layout

