\family typewriter
vertex
\family default
).
 An STL file can contain multiple 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

solids
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, whereby each solid must be labeled such that it can be distinguished from
 others.
 In the above example, the shown triangles belong to a solid named 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

WALL_VALVE_INTAKE_CONE
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 which, alongside other solids, is stored within an STL file containing
 all 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

moving
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 intake valve surfaces (described later).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Surface-wrapping is by far the most elaborate part in setting up a particular
 engine configuration.
 It is also one of the most delicate tasks, since bad surface-wrapping will
 undoubtedly lead to poor computational results at best or - at worst and
 more likely - solver or applet crashes.
 Hence, before turning to more practical instructions for eninge configuration
 creation, some useful basic surface-wrapping techniques will be covered.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Surface-Wrapping Software
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Surface-wrapping in general represents a sequence of basic CAD tasks to
 simplify, break up, recombine or remodel (reverse-engineer) particular
 sections or parts of a given geometry.
 As such, a general-purpose CAD software like FreeCAD proves particularly
 useful, since it it relatively easy to use, freely available (and open-source),
 and grants the user thorough control over the surface triangulations/STL
 that need to be created.
 However, other CAD software should also be suitable to follow this guide
 as long as it allows for the here needed manipulations of the STL geometry.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Sub-Division and Recombination of Geometry Components
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In practice it makes sense, to sub-divide each of the above components into
 smaller bits that allow, for example, for precise edge capturing and local
 resolution control in the later meshing process.
 Furthermore, features of components of the same type - such as two identical
 intake valves in a four-valve engine - can be recombined as a single sub-compon
ent although there is no physical connection between those features - cf.,
 e.g., the two shafts of the to intake valves.
 To better illustrate the practical meaning of sub-division and recombination
 of sub-components, consider the just mentioned pair of intake valves as
 displayed in Figure .
 Both valves are identical by design and move in perfect alignment during
 the engine cycle.
 For both valves, especially the valve plates consist of prominent features
 like chamfers etc.
 that should be captured adequately during mesh generation.
 To enable the used mesher
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Here: 
\family typewriter
cartesianMesh
\family default
 or 
\family typewriter
pMesh
\family default
 of the cfMesh module incorporated in ESI's more recent OpenFOAM versions
\end_layout

\end_inset

 to detect those features at reasonable accuracy and quality, the bottom
 section of the valves should thus be sub-divided into corresponding parts,
 as demonstrated in Figure .
 As can be seen, the valve geometries are sub-divided into functional geometric
 bits, preferably at natural boundaries such as steps or corners.
 Note that such decompositions force the mesher to introduce adequate surface
 mesh boundaries as well which, in turn, generally leads to well-captured
 steps and corners in the final mesh.
 Conversely, the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

twin
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

 features of both valves are at the same time recombined into a single STL
 entity, like the valve shafts (purple) or plate edges (green).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Spatial Resolution Requirements
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to faithfully capture important geometric features, especially
 if they are small in size but strong in impact, it must thus be ensured
 that the resulting triangulation resolution is always fine enough to
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
capture design-relevant shapes (e.g.
 spark plug electrode gap) correctly and
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
closes the domain such that the mesher considers it as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

watertight
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, meaning the resulting local mesh cell size will always be significantly
 larger than local surface-wrapping-induced gaps between surfaces/edges
 or other geometric inaccuracies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Simplification of Irrelevant Geometric Features
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
werwe
\end_layout

